{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YL4FU2jBwduHGCno8hyotg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 310ms at APC 300 (2.74x increase), despite total work (cells, constraints, bus messages) decreasing by >2x. This is the second-largest contributor to poor scaling after Round 0 (addressed in the previous task).\n8\t\n9\t**Root cause**: The `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:793-912` iterates over \"windows\" (one per trace matrix), launching a separate kernel per window. For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces **10,348 single-block kernel launches** at APC 300 (vs 3,281 at APC 0).\n10\t\n11\tEach degenerate window launch involves:\n12\t1. `d_accum.fill_zero()` — a separate memset kernel\n13\t2. H2D copy of `eq_ub_slice` (~1-2us)\n14\t3. Degenerate kernel launch with `grid(1), block(min(window_len, 256))` — avg 3.9us GPU time\n15\t4. `d_accum.to_host()` — D2H copy + implicit sync\n16\t\n17\tNsight profiling confirms: GPU kernel time for all stacked reduction kernels at APC 300 is ~93ms, but wall-clock is 310ms. **~217ms (70%) is CPU-side launch/sync overhead** from issuing 10,348+ tiny kernel launches sequentially.\n18\t\n19\tKey code locations:\n20\t- Rust loop: `stacked_reduction.rs:833-907` (the `for window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` loop)\n21\t- CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction.cu:306-368` (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n22\t- CUDA launcher: `stacked_reduction.cu:537-570` (launches with `grid(1)`)\n23\t\n24\t## Proposed Approach\n25\t\n26\tBatch all degenerate windows within each call to `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` into a single kernel launch. This is safe because:\n27\t- Windows are independent (no data dependencies between them)\n28\t- All window results are summed at the end (line 909-911: `s_evals_batch.iter().map(|evals| evals[i]).sum()`)\n29\t- The kernel already uses atomic accumulation (`atomic_add_fpext_to_u64`)\n30\t- Overflow is not a risk: 10,348 blocks x ~2^31 max per component < 2^45 << 2^64\n31\t\n32\t**Implementation plan:**\n33\t\n34\t1. **New batched CUDA kernel** (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel`):\n35\t   - Grid: `(num_degenerate_windows)` — one block per window\n36\t   - Each block loads per-window metadata from a device array: window offset into `unstacked_cols`/`lambda_pows`, window length, `eq_r`, `k_rot_r`\n37\t   - Per-thread computation is identical to the current kernel\n38\t   - All blocks atomically add to a single shared accumulator\n39\t\n40\t2. **Rust-side changes** in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()`:\n41\t   - First pass: separate degenerate vs non-degenerate windows\n42\t   - Degenerate path: batch all eq_ub values into one H2D copy, build per-window metadata, launch single batched kernel, single D2H copy\n43\t   - Non-degenerate path: keep existing per-window launch (these are larger and already auto-tuned)\n44\t\n45\t3. **Data layout for batched kernel**:\n46\t   - `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` are already contiguous on device — just pass window offsets\n47\t   - Pre-upload concatenated `eq_ub` values in one H2D transfer\n48\t   - Per-window metadata struct: `{ offset: u32, len: u32, eq_r: FpExt, k_rot_r: FpExt }`\n49\t\n50\t## Expected Outcome\n51\t\n52\t- **Stacked Reduction APC 300**: ~310ms -> ~110-130ms (save ~180-200ms)\n53\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 kernel launches -> 1 launch per round (save ~100-150ms of CPU launch overhead)\n54\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 fill_zero calls -> 1 memset (save ~10ms)\n55\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 D2H copies -> 1 D2H (save ~10-20ms)\n56\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 H2D eq_ub copies -> 1 H2D (save ~10-20ms)\n57\t- **Stacked Reduction APC 0**: Minimal change (~113ms, only 3,281 launches — less overhead to begin with)\n58\t- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: ~2358ms -> ~2160-2180ms (cumulative with previous task: ~300ms vs baseline)\n59\t- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0): ~1.086 -> ~1.00-1.02 (near parity)\n60\t\n61\t## Success Criteria\n62\t\n63\t1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 drops below 150ms (>50% reduction from 310ms)\n64\t2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 drops below 2200ms (>10% reduction from baseline 2478ms)\n65\t3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio drops below 1.05\n66\t4. **Correctness**: All `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n67\t\n68\t## Considered Alternatives\n69\t\n70\t1. **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2** (~30-50ms savings at APC 300, High probability, Simple complexity). Chosen against because: lower absolute impact than Stacked Reduction batching. Good follow-up task.\n71\t\n72\t2. **Multi-stream pipelining for Round 0** (~50-100ms savings, Medium probability, Complex). Chosen against because: complex synchronization with uncertain overlap benefit. Higher risk.\n73\t\n74\t3. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (~30-50ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: non-degenerate kernels already do more work per launch (auto-tuned grid.y), so per-launch overhead is proportionally smaller. Could be added as an enhancement within this task if the degenerate batching succeeds.\n75\t\n76\t4. **Batch MLE round kernel launches** (~20-30ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: smaller absolute impact than Stacked Reduction. Less clear batching opportunity.\n77\t\n78\tStacked Reduction batching was selected because it has the highest (expected impact x probability / complexity): ~180ms savings with high confidence (windows are provably independent, all results are summed) and moderate implementation effort (new kernel + loop restructuring, following the proven pattern from the Round 0 optimization).\n79\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd9f14b9-5260-4636-ab39-a271be4bf43b","uuid":"9ec1b632-c1f6-42c3-8f6f-aaa065ac8108","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:22:56.743Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n\nTask name: 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 310ms at APC 300 (2.74x increase), despite total work (cells, constraints, bus messages) decreasing by >2x. This is the second-largest contributor to poor scaling after Round 0 (addressed in the previous task).\n\n**Root cause**: The `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:793-912` iterates over \"windows\" (one per trace matrix), launching a separate kernel per window. For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces **10,348 single-block kernel launches** at APC 300 (vs 3,281 at APC 0).\n\nEach degenerate window launch involves:\n1. `d_accum.fill_zero()` — a separate memset kernel\n2. H2D copy of `eq_ub_slice` (~1-2us)\n3. Degenerate kernel launch with `grid(1), block(min(window_len, 256))` — avg 3.9us GPU time\n4. `d_accum.to_host()` — D2H copy + implicit sync\n\nNsight profiling confirms: GPU kernel time for all stacked reduction kernels at APC 300 is ~93ms, but wall-clock is 310ms. **~217ms (70%) is CPU-side launch/sync overhead** from issuing 10,348+ tiny kernel launches sequentially.\n\nKey code locations:\n- Rust loop: `stacked_reduction.rs:833-907` (the `for window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` loop)\n- CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction.cu:306-368` (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n- CUDA launcher: `stacked_reduction.cu:537-570` (launches with `grid(1)`)\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nBatch all degenerate windows within each call to `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` into a single kernel launch. This is safe because:\n- Windows are independent (no data dependencies between them)\n- All window results are summed at the end (line 909-911: `s_evals_batch.iter().map(|evals| evals[i]).sum()`)\n- The kernel already uses atomic accumulation (`atomic_add_fpext_to_u64`)\n- Overflow is not a risk: 10,348 blocks x ~2^31 max per component < 2^45 << 2^64\n\n**Implementation plan:**\n\n1. **New batched CUDA kernel** (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel`):\n   - Grid: `(num_degenerate_windows)` — one block per window\n   - Each block loads per-window metadata from a device array: window offset into `unstacked_cols`/`lambda_pows`, window length, `eq_r`, `k_rot_r`\n   - Per-thread computation is identical to the current kernel\n   - All blocks atomically add to a single shared accumulator\n\n2. **Rust-side changes** in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()`:\n   - First pass: separate degenerate vs non-degenerate windows\n   - Degenerate path: batch all eq_ub values into one H2D copy, build per-window metadata, launch single batched kernel, single D2H copy\n   - Non-degenerate path: keep existing per-window launch (these are larger and already auto-tuned)\n\n3. **Data layout for batched kernel**:\n   - `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` are already contiguous on device — just pass window offsets\n   - Pre-upload concatenated `eq_ub` values in one H2D transfer\n   - Per-window metadata struct: `{ offset: u32, len: u32, eq_r: FpExt, k_rot_r: FpExt }`\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **Stacked Reduction APC 300**: ~310ms -> ~110-130ms (save ~180-200ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 kernel launches -> 1 launch per round (save ~100-150ms of CPU launch overhead)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 fill_zero calls -> 1 memset (save ~10ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 D2H copies -> 1 D2H (save ~10-20ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 H2D eq_ub copies -> 1 H2D (save ~10-20ms)\n- **Stacked Reduction APC 0**: Minimal change (~113ms, only 3,281 launches — less overhead to begin with)\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: ~2358ms -> ~2160-2180ms (cumulative with previous task: ~300ms vs baseline)\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0): ~1.086 -> ~1.00-1.02 (near parity)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 drops below 150ms (>50% reduction from 310ms)\n2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 drops below 2200ms (>10% reduction from baseline 2478ms)\n3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio drops below 1.05\n4. **Correctness**: All `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2** (~30-50ms savings at APC 300, High probability, Simple complexity). Chosen against because: lower absolute impact than Stacked Reduction batching. Good follow-up task.\n\n2. **Multi-stream pipelining for Round 0** (~50-100ms savings, Medium probability, Complex). Chosen against because: complex synchronization with uncertain overlap benefit. Higher risk.\n\n3. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (~30-50ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: non-degenerate kernels already do more work per launch (auto-tuned grid.y), so per-launch overhead is proportionally smaller. Could be added as an enhancement within this task if the degenerate batching succeeds.\n\n4. **Batch MLE round kernel launches** (~20-30ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: smaller absolute impact than Stacked Reduction. Less clear batching opportunity.\n\nStacked Reduction batching was selected because it has the highest (expected impact x probability / complexity): ~180ms savings with high confidence (windows are provably independent, all results are summed) and moderate implementation effort (new kernel + loop restructuring, following the proven pattern from the Round 0 optimization).\n","numLines":79,"startLine":1,"totalLines":79}}}
